# Unsupervised Anomaly Detection in Multi-Wavelength Astronomical Imaging Using Convolutional Autoencoders

**Project:** Astro-Anomaly-Detector  
**Dataset:** Hybrid-Astro-Deblender (HST + AstroSat multi-wavelength tiles)  
**Date:** March 2026  
**Type:** Standalone Research Module — Unsupervised Deep Learning

---

## Abstract

This work presents an unsupervised anomaly detection framework for multi-wavelength astronomical image data, built as a companion module to the Hybrid-Astro-Deblender pipeline. A Convolutional Autoencoder (CAE) is trained exclusively on normal 128×128 pixel, 4-channel astronomical tiles derived from Hubble Space Telescope (HST) and AstroSat observations. The model learns a compressed latent representation (256-dimensional bottleneck) of the standard tile distribution. At inference, tiles that deviate significantly from learned patterns — such as cosmic ray hits, blended source objects, sensor artifacts, transient events, and resolved extended galaxies — produce abnormally high reconstruction errors, which are quantified into a combined anomaly score. Across a validation set of **3,362 tiles**, the system detected **101 anomalies (3.0%)** using a 95th-percentile threshold after a full **60-epoch** training run. The combined scoring metric (MSE + SSIM-Δ, each weighted at 0.5) produced anomaly scores ranging from **0.041** (most normal) to **0.413** (most anomalous), with a mean of **0.193**. The pipeline is fully automated across three stages — training, detection, and visualization — and produces ranked anomaly reports with spatial heatmaps and multi-panel diagnostic visualizations. This approach demonstrates that reconstruction-error-based anomaly detection is a viable, label-free technique for identifying rare or unexpected phenomena in large-scale astronomical surveys.

---

## 1. Introduction

### 1.1 Background

Modern astronomical surveys generate enormous volumes of imaging data. Large-scale sky surveys using instruments such as the Hubble Space Telescope (HST) and multi-wavelength observatories like AstroSat produce tens of thousands to millions of image tiles per observation campaign. Manual inspection of each tile is infeasible at scale. Consequently, automated techniques capable of distinguishing ordinary sky regions from observationally unusual or scientifically significant detections are of great importance.

Anomaly detection in this context refers to the identification of tiles that contain phenomena that significantly deviate from the statistical baseline of "normal" astronomical imagery. These anomalies may include:

| Category | Description |
|---|---|
| **Cosmic Ray Hits** | Linear streaks or bright spikes from high-energy charged particles impacting the detector |
| **Blended Sources** | Two overlapping galaxies or stars merging into a single over-bright region |
| **Sensor Artifacts** | Hot pixels, column bleed, charge traps, or read-out anomalies |
| **Transient Events** | Supernovae, asteroid trails, or active variable star outbursts |
| **Extended Galaxies** | Resolved, morphologically complex galaxies distinct from dominant point sources |

### 1.2 Motivation and Context

This project is a complementary, standalone research component built on top of the **Hybrid-Astro-Deblender** pipeline, which performs source detection and star-galaxy separation on multi-wavelength image data. Whereas the deblender pipeline focuses on characterizing known source types, this module asks the orthogonal question: *"Which tiles look fundamentally different from everything else?"*

The key motivation for using an unsupervised autoencoder approach is the absence of labeled anomaly data. In real astronomical surveys, anomalies by definition are rare and diverse — it is neither practical nor reliable to hand-label every class of anomaly for supervised training. Reconstruction-error-based anomaly detection sidesteps this labeling requirement entirely.

### 1.3 Goals and Contributions

The specific goals of this work are:
1. To train a Convolutional Autoencoder on normal tile data from existing multi-wavelength observations.
2. To score every validation tile against the learned reconstruction quality.
3. To rank, threshold, and visualize anomalous tiles in a reproducible, interpretable manner.
4. To produce a fully automated, configuration-driven pipeline from data ingestion to anomaly reporting.

